--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ideas, techniques and constants borrowed from other projects
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

No source code from the projects below is copied into this repository. What is
reused are published techniques and numeric constants — facts, not expression —
and they are credited here because that is the decent thing to do.

* Cap — https://github.com/CapSoftware/Cap (MIT)
  The spring constants for the camera and cursor come from its
  `crates/project/src/configuration.rs`: stiffness/damping/mass triples for the
  screen-movement spring and the four cursor animation styles, the 500 ms click
  look-ahead, the contract-to-80%-over-130 ms click feedback, the 0.25 edge-snap
  ratio and the click-cluster box ratios. See `scripts/bake-springs.ts`.

* driver.js — https://github.com/nilbuild/driver.js (MIT)
  The spotlight technique: an SVG path with a cut-out and `fill-rule: evenodd`,
  rather than a large `box-shadow`. Reimplemented in `overlay/src/spotlight.ts`.

* ghost-cursor — https://github.com/Xetera/ghost-cursor (MIT)
  Fitts's-Law travel timing for the synthetic cursor. Its randomised jitter and
  in-target offset are deliberately NOT used: those exist to defeat bot
  detection, which is the opposite of what a demo needs.

* Screenity — https://github.com/alyssaxuu/screenity (GPL-3.0)
  Read for reference only. Two of its published constants informed defaults, and
  two of its bugs are deliberately avoided (documented in
  `overlay/src/cursor.ts`). No code from it is present here.

* shot-scraper — https://github.com/simonw/shot-scraper (Apache-2.0)
  The shape of its storyboard action vocabulary.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Runtime dependencies invoked as subprocesses
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

* gpu-screen-recorder (GPL-3.0-only) — invoked as a subprocess via the `rec`
  wrapper. It is never linked against, and no GPL code is included here.

* ffmpeg / ffprobe — invoked as subprocesses for audio trimming, loudness
  normalisation and container inspection.
